  "statement": "I concede that much of the current industry-led safety effort is performative, but replacing it with the static, bureaucratic gatekeeping of the nuclear age is a category error that ignores the fundamental nature of intelligence. Safetyist’s reliance on formal verification models—developed for rigid, physical systems like aviation—fails when applied to iterative, information-based AI. These models treat software as a static product rather than a dynamic system, creating a dangerous deployment-capability lag that sacrifices real-world progress for the illusion of control. We do not stop the evolution of intelligence by locking it in a hangar; we ensure its safety by iterating through continuous, high-fidelity telemetry that exposes emergent behaviors in real-time.\n\nFormal verification is a brittle trap that assumes we can map the entire state space of a frontier model before it ever interacts with the world. This is not how software, or intelligence, actually scales. By demanding mathematical proofs for systems characterized by non-linear, emergent behaviors, regulators create a structural bottleneck that effectively halts innovation, leading to the measurable loss of life we see when superior diagnostic or defensive capabilities are withheld from the public. We must move beyond the 'pre-deployment' myth, as no static snapshot can capture the risks of an adaptive system operating in an open, complex environment.\n\nInstead of performative gatekeeping, we should implement a model of market-based accountability that aligns corporate incentives with safety performance. When developers are directly liable for the real-world outcomes of their systems, they develop internal oversight mechanisms far more rigorous than any checkbox-driven regulatory regime. By shifting the burden from ex-ante permission to ex-post accountability, we foster a competitive landscape where safety becomes a premium feature rather than a regulatory hurdle. This approach treats AI as the iterative, information-based technology it is, ensuring that our oversight mechanisms evolve at the speed of the intelligence they are meant to govern.",
